= Albert Einstein
{c}
{parent=List of physicists}
{title2=1879-1955}
{wiki}

= A. Einstein
{c}
{synonym}

A <physicist> whose work connects <special relativity>, <general relativity>, <Brownian motion> and the <photoelectric effect>. He worked at the Institute for Advanced Study from 1933 until his death in 1955.<Albert Einstein (institutional profile) by Institute for Advanced Study>[Institutional profile: physicist, life dates and Institute appointment]{ref}

In 1905, <Albert Einstein> used the <photoelectric effect> to explore quantized <light>, related <Brownian motion> to molecular agitation, formulated <special relativity>, and connected changes in <mass> and <energy>. These problems link visible measurements to otherwise inaccessible structure: emitted <electron> energies, suspended-particle displacements and comparisons of clocks.<Albert Einstein: The Great Works by Center for History of Physics>[March, May, June and September 1905: the four papers and their physical questions]{ref}

The <Millikan photoelectric experiment> follows one of those predictions into the laboratory. Its stopping-voltage measurements test a quantitative relation; the experiment's apparatus, surface preparation and electrical corrections matter to that inference. The <Einstein field equations> and <general relativity> lead to a different set of tests involving <gravity> and <spacetime>.

The <Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921> recognized his theoretical work, particularly the law of the <photoelectric effect>; the award was presented in 1922.<Albert Einstein, Facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Prize motivation and the note that Einstein received the 1921 award in 1922]{ref}

\Image[https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Albert_Einstein_Head.jpg]
{title=Albert Einstein in 1947, Library of Congress portrait via Wikimedia Commons}
{width=240}

= Work by Albert Einstein
{parent=Albert Einstein}

= Erwin Schrödinger
{c}
{parent=List of physicists}
{title2=1887-1961}
{wiki}

A <physicist> who developed <wave mechanics>. In 1926, <Erwin Schrödinger> made the energies of <chemical atoms> an <eigenvalue> problem, rather than prescribing a separate quantization rule for each classical <orbit>. He shared the <Nobel Prize in Physics in 1933> with <Paul Dirac>.<Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941/Erwin Schrödinger - Biographical>[Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941; Zurich period and 1926 wave-equation work; joint 1933 award; birth and death dates]{ref}

The <Schrödinger equation> turns a proposed <Hamiltonian operator> into predictions for a <wavefunction> and its evolution. <Boundary conditions> can select discrete <energy eigenvalues>, while <quantum superposition> makes their relative <quantum phases> observable through <interference>. The <infinite square well> gives a transparent worked example; a <hydrogen atom> supplies a physical application with a different <potential energy>.

The <Davisson–Germer experiment> gives a complementary experimental route into <matter waves>: an accelerated <electron> beam scattered from a <nickel> crystal produces direction-dependent collected <electric current>. Its apparatus and angular scans make the wave prediction testable. Interpreting a <wavefunction> as a <probability amplitude> also requires the <Born rule>; that statistical interpretation deserves its own history alongside <Erwin Schrödinger>'s dynamics.

\Image[https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Erwin_Schrödinger_(1933).jpg]
{title=Erwin Schrödinger in 1933, Nobel Foundation portrait via Wikimedia Commons}
{width=240}

= Work by Erwin Schrödinger
{parent=Erwin Schrödinger}

